{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789957044,"nonce":"4SOWGsuPopg4nuRRdth0Cmq95NE=","title":"Lua conformance: stdlib/os.expected pins the macOS spelling of %Z","message":"crates/zyntax_lua/conformance/stdlib/os.lua prints `os.date(\"!... %Z\", 86400 * 40)`. The pinned os.expected (made on macOS) has `UTC` on that line; glibc on x86_64 Linux prints `GMT`, and both lua5.4 and zylua print `GMT` there. So `cargo test -p zyntax_lua --test conformance` reports `stdlib/os.lua` as a regression on the NUC (16 passed, 1 failed) while zylua and the reference agree on the machine. Not a compiler bug. Either drop `%Z` from the case (it is the one libc-dependent conversion in it) or pin per platform.","files":null}]}